   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the vehicle and traffic law, in relation to establishing a distinctive \"Jamaican bobsled team\" number plate",
    "basePrintNo": "S4019",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S4019-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §404-y, V & T L",
    "lawSection": "Vehicle and Traffic Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S4019\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the vehicle and traffic law, in\nrelation to establishing a distinctive \"Jamaican bobsled team\" number\nplate\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nThe purpose of this legislation is to allow the issuance of a\ndistinctive Jamaican Bobsled team member plate of a design developed\nand approved by the commissioner, in cooperation with Jamaica Bobsled\nfoundation.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection one of the bill adds § 404-y to the vehicle and traffic law to\nprovide any resident of New York State to, upon request, be issued a\ndistinctive \"Jamaican Bobsled team\" number plate of a design developed\nand approved by the commissioner in cooperation with the Jamaican\nBobsled Foundation. The distribution of a \"Jamaican Bobsled team\"\nnumber plate shall be issued in the same manner as other number plates\nupon the payment of regular registration fees. An annual service\ncharge of twenty-five dollars will be charged for such a plate and\nwill be deposited pursuant to the provisions of section four hundred\nfour-oo to the plate development fund of the department of motor\nvehicles. The State Comptroller shall pay annual service charges\ncollected in excess of six thousand dollars to the Jamaica Bobsled\nFoundation. Section two of the bill provides the effective date.\n\n EXISTING LAW :\n\nNone\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nThe Jamaican Bobsled team was first formed in preparation for the 1988\nWinter Olympics in Calgary, Alberta. They were immediately seen as\npopular underdogs being from a tropical island and competing in a\nnotorious winter sporting event. The Jamaican Bobsled legacy continues\nafter several more appearances in subsequent Winter Olympics and\nstunned many of their critics by finishing in 14th place in the 1994\nOlympic Games, finishing ahead of Russia, the United States,\nAustralia, and France. The creation and sale of a \"Jamaica Bobsled\nteam\" designed license plate would benefit the team as it aims to\ngenerate support and popularity through the promotion with proceeds\ngoing towards the Jamaican Bobsled Foundation spearheaded by Executive\nDirector, Noel Spencer, and one of the inaugural Olympic team members,\nDevon Harris.  With many Jamaican immigrants residing in the New York,\nthe establishment of this plate would provide residents an opportunity\nto support and fund the country's team in its training and journey\nback to the Olympics.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\n2015-2016: A.7519/S.5364 - Referred to Transportation / Transportation\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone to the State.\n\n LOCAL FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nImmediately.\n",
