    "lawCode": "Add §404-ee, V & T L",
    "lawSection": "Vehicle and Traffic Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S4019A\n\nSPONSOR: HAMILTON\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the vehicle and traffic law, in\nrelation to establishing a distinctive \"Jamaican bobsled team\" number\nplate\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThe purpose of this legislation is to allow for the issuance of a\ndistinctive Jamaican Bobsled team number plate of a design developed and\napproved by the commissioner.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one of the bill adds § 404-z to the vehicle and traffic law\nauthorizing the issuance of distinctive \"Jamaican Bobsled team\" license\nplates. Subdivision one provides that any resident of New York State to,\nupon request, be issued a distinctive \"Jamaican Bobsled team\" license\nplate of a design developed and approved by the commissioner. Subdivi-\nsion two provides that the distribution of a \"Jamaican Bobsled team\"\nlicense plate shall be issued in the same manner as other number plates\nupon the payment of regular registration fees and an additional annual\n$25.00 fee.\n\nSection two of the bill provides that a distinctive plate shall only be\ndesigned, produced, and issued upon the delivery of a surety bond in the\namount of $6,000 or at least 200 orders for such distinctive plate with\nthe additional one-time service charge applicable to each order. All of\nthe service charges will be deposited into the DMV distinctive place\ndevelopment fund. The language also provides that if after two years\nfollowing the date upon which the plate is first made available for\nsale, 200 or more sets of plates are sold, and the bond shall be discon-\ntinued. If fewer than 200 plates are sold within the first 2 years, the\nDMV shall be entitled to recover against the bond in the amount propor-\ntionate to the short fall.\n\nSection three of the bill provides the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe Jamaican Bobsled team was first formed in in preparation for the\n1988 Winter Olympics in Calgary, Alberta. They were immediately seen as\npopular underdogs being from a tropical island and competing in a noto-\nrious winter sporting event. The Jamaican Bobsled legacy continued after\nseveral more appearances in subsequent Winter Olympics and stunned many\nof their critics by finishing in 14th place in the 1994 Olympic Games,\nfinishing ahead of Russia, the United States, Australia, and France. The\ncreation and sale of a \"Jamaica Bobsled team\" designed license plate\naims to generate popularity through the promotion. With many Jamaican\nimmigrants residing in the New York, the establishment of this plate\nwould provide residents an opportunity to support the country's team\njourney back to the Olympics.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2015-2016: A.7519/S.5364 - Referred to Transportation / Transportation\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\nNone to the State.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately, provided, however, that section one shall take effect one\nhundred eighty days after becoming a law. Effective immediately, the\naddition, amendment and/or repeal of any rule or regulation necessary\nfor the implementation of this act on its effective date are authorized\nand directed to be made and completed on or before such effective date.\n",
